1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,177
Here is a loop that walks a tree.

2
00:00:03,227 --> 00:00:06,551
It takes a node, visits
it, and adds its children.

3
00:00:06,601 --> 00:00:10,869
Take from the same end you add
to, and it dives down one branch.

4
00:00:10,919 --> 00:00:16,470
Change one word, to take from the other
end, and it sweeps the tree level by level.

5
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:19,152
Level order traversal.

6
00:00:19,202 --> 00:00:21,884
You are given the root of a binary tree.

7
00:00:21,934 --> 00:00:27,279
Return its values grouped by depth: one
list for each level, from the top down,

8
00:00:27,329 --> 00:00:28,850
and from left to right.

9
00:00:29,367 --> 00:00:33,024
The brute force way: for each
depth, walk the whole tree,

10
00:00:33,074 --> 00:00:36,341
and collect the nodes at
that depth. It is correct.

11
00:00:36,391 --> 00:00:39,073
But every pass starts from the root again,

12
00:00:39,123 --> 00:00:41,935
so the same nodes are
visited over and over.

13
00:00:41,985 --> 00:00:45,317
On a tree that is one long
chain, that is quadratic.

14
00:00:46,467 --> 00:00:48,694
The better idea is to never go back.

15
00:00:48,744 --> 00:00:52,743
Keep a queue of nodes that have
been found, but not visited yet.

16
00:00:52,793 --> 00:00:57,045
Take one from the front, visit it,
and add its children at the back.

17
00:00:57,095 --> 00:01:01,537
Nodes come out in the order they went
in: top to bottom, left to right.

18
00:01:02,067 --> 00:01:03,514
One question is left.

19
00:01:03,564 --> 00:01:06,722
Where does one level
end, and the next begin?

20
00:01:06,772 --> 00:01:09,858
Look at the queue just
before a pass starts.

21
00:01:09,908 --> 00:01:13,066
It holds exactly one
level, and nothing else.

22
00:01:13,116 --> 00:01:16,417
So its length tells you
how many nodes to take.

23
00:01:17,667 --> 00:01:18,192
In code.

24
00:01:18,242 --> 00:01:20,423
The queue starts with the root.

25
00:01:20,473 --> 00:01:24,668
Its length is one, so the first
pass takes one node: three.

26
00:01:24,718 --> 00:01:29,777
Its children, nine and twenty, go to
the back. The first level is done.

27
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:34,341
Now the queue holds two nodes:
the whole second level.

28
00:01:34,391 --> 00:01:36,918
Take nine, and add four and seven.

29
00:01:36,968 --> 00:01:39,115
Take twenty, and add fifteen.

30
00:01:39,165 --> 00:01:42,070
Two nodes were taken, so the pass ends.

31
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:44,570
The second level is nine, twenty.

32
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:50,885
Three nodes are waiting, so this pass
takes three. Four has no children.

33
00:01:50,935 --> 00:01:53,722
Seven adds one. Fifteen adds eight.

34
00:01:53,772 --> 00:01:57,450
That is the third level:
four, seven, fifteen.

35
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,317
Two nodes are left: one and eight.

36
00:02:00,367 --> 00:02:03,240
Neither has children, so nothing is added.

37
00:02:03,290 --> 00:02:08,670
The queue is empty, the loop ends, and
the result has one list for each level.

38
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:11,353
Now the edge cases.

39
00:02:11,403 --> 00:02:16,336
An empty tree: return an empty
list, before touching the queue.

40
00:02:16,386 --> 00:02:18,313
A single node: one level.

41
00:02:18,363 --> 00:02:23,850
And a tree that is one long chain:
every level holds exactly one node.

42
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:31,265
Time: every node enters the queue once,
and leaves it once. That is linear: O(n).

43
00:02:31,315 --> 00:02:35,942
Space: the queue never holds
more than two levels at a time,

44
00:02:35,992 --> 00:02:40,230
so the extra space is
proportional to the widest level.

45
00:02:41,367 --> 00:02:42,635
Where is this used?

46
00:02:42,685 --> 00:02:44,646
This is breadth first search.

47
00:02:44,696 --> 00:02:48,738
The same loop finds the shortest
path in a maze or a graph.

48
00:02:48,788 --> 00:02:54,357
And it solves zigzag traversal, right side
view, and the minimum depth of a tree.

49
00:02:55,400 --> 00:03:00,973
And if you take from the other end instead,
you get depth first search. Same loop.

50
00:03:01,023 --> 00:03:01,590
One word.

51
00:03:03,233 --> 00:03:04,420
Thanks for watching.

52
00:03:04,470 --> 00:03:08,809
Subscribe to Edge Cases for more
Python, algorithms, and system design,

53
00:03:08,859 --> 00:03:09,983
explained visually.
